crypto: an interrupt or an error in the inner read is no end of the input

crypto_read() took every inner read that returned nothing or an error for
the end of the encrypted input: it set eof, decrypted what it held as the
input's last data and took the last plaintext byte for the PKCS7 padding
count, dropping that many bytes (up to 255) of real data, or all it held
when the count was larger, and ended. When the caller's interrupt
callback stops the inner http read (AVERROR_EXIT), an AES-128 segment of
hls ended there, short of the rest and of those bytes: 0099 reads an
unencrypted segment on after an interrupt, an encrypted one lost the rest.

Return AVERROR_EXIT as it is. Nothing was taken from the inner input, and
what was read before stays in the input buffer, so the next read goes on
from there. So is every other error (a body cut short, which 0107 ends
in EIO): it was taken for the end too, the cut segment shortened by a
"padding" count read from its data and played with no error logged. Only
the end of the input (a read of 0, AVERROR_EOF) ends it.

diff --git a/libavformat/crypto.c b/libavformat/crypto.c
--- a/libavformat/crypto.c
+++ b/libavformat/crypto.c
@@ -199,6 +199,11 @@ retry:
     while (c->indata - c->indata_used < 2*BLOCKSIZE) {
         int n = ffurl_read(c->hd, c->inbuffer + c->indata,
                            sizeof(c->inbuffer) - c->indata);
+        /* The caller's interrupt callback is no end, nor is an error (a cut
+         * body, 0107): what was read stays in inbuffer, an interrupted read
+         * goes on from there on the next call. */
+        if (n < 0 && n != AVERROR_EOF)
+            return n;
         if (n <= 0) {
             c->eof = 1;
             break;
